/bin/newuser from the Micronix 1.6 distribution

There is no source for newuser, so this is the disassembly, and newuser.c is
that disassembly read back into C.

    disas -f newuser.ctl newuser.dist

newuser.dist is the binary off inode 29 of disks/dist/1012-8_dist_2.IMD -
the second distribution disk, not the first, which is where login and the
rest of the early commands are - 17526 bytes, mode 4711, owner root, dated
7 August 1982.  It is stripped.  It is identical to the copy that has sat
in filesystem/bin all along.

    src/tools/mnix -f disks/dist/1012-8_dist_2.IMD cat /bin/newuser


the binary has no header
------------------------

Every other Whitesmiths binary here begins 99 94, the object magic, and a
sixteen byte header that says where the text and data go.  This one begins
c1 21 00 00 39, which is code: pop bc; ld hl,0; add hl,sp.  The kernel's
exec (sys/exec.c) has a case for that - a file whose first word is not the
magic is "old cpm format", and the whole file is loaded as text at 0100 and
entered there, with no data segment and no bss.  So the text is 0100 to
4576 and the library's data - its stdio pool, the errno cell, the register
variable cells - is the tail of the same image.  size(1) refuses the file
for the same reason, and disas has to be told nothing at all.

The file ends in the system call blocks - 44ee to 455c, a call number and
room for the arguments each, the last of them cf 04 for write - and not in a
relocation table; there is nothing left to apply.


the runtime
-----------

The same as login's and init's: Whitesmith's, arguments pushed right to
left, so arg0 is on top, results in BC.

    3fc4  c.ent    ld hl,0; add hl,sp; ex de,hl; ex (sp),hl; jp (hl)
    3fcb  c.ret    ex de,hl; ld sp,hl; pop de; ret
    3b03  c.ents   the same, and saves the three register variable cells
    3b19  c.rets   and puts them back

The cells are 4570, 4572 and 4574, which the control file names r2, r3 and
r4, as login.ctl does for its own.  A function that opens with CALL c.ents
is one that has a register variable, and that is how newuser's own functions
are told from its helpers at a glance: the ones that walk a list keep the
pointer in r4.

System calls are not stubs that load a number; the stubs are rst 8 with the
call number after it and the arguments laid out in a block in the data -
syscall 0 and an indirect block, in the old way.  The numbers are the V7
ones and are in the table of the kernel's: 3768 access (33), 379f chown
(16), 37c2 close (6), 37f5 exit (1), 37fb getuid (24), 3820 link (9), 3843
mknod (14), 3871 open (5), 3895 read (3), 38c1 seek (19), 38ec signal (48),
3a6f sleep (35), 3a7d stat (18), 3aa0 time (13), 3abf unlink (10), 3ad7
write (4).

The exit at 2b54 closes the sixteen stdio slots and then calls 37f5, which
passes its argument to the kernel as it is.  That is the whole of why the
exit status is the other way round - see below.


the layout
----------

0100  the startup: pops argc, computes argv, pushes both, calls main at 010e,
      passes what it returns to exit.
010e  main.  Three calls and an exit.
0122  newuser's own text, with its strings interleaved as the compiler wrote
      them, to 15ed.
15ee  getpwent, fld, getpwuid, getpwnam, setpwent, endpwent - libS.a's
      passwd reader, byte for byte the module login carries at 0f0b..119b
      (login.ctl, getpwent.c).  freopen, fopen, getfile, openfile, finit,
      fclose follow at 187d and the stdio layer goes on from there, then
      malloc, calloc, exit, perror, atoi, the string and block routines, the
      system call stubs, and the arithmetic helpers to 3fe6.
3ff6  the end of the code.  The data follows to the end of the file: newuser's
      own globals first (the passwd and group lists and their files, at 3ff6
      to 401f), then the library's - the stdio pool at 4392, errno at 455e,
      the register variable cells, the system call blocks.

newuser's own functions, in address order.  The names are what each does:

    010e  main          rootonly, then newuser, then exit(1)
    016f  getname       ask until goodname
    01a5  newuser       getname, adduser
    01c2  goodname      two or more characters, all a to z
    021a  userexists    findname, as a truth value
    0264  removeuser    UNREFERENCED - "Removing <name> from the system.  OK? "
    03a5  adduser       the whole job
    056c  addpw         copy an entry into the list
    0681  readpw        getpwent into the list
    06b0  strsave       malloc and copy
    0709  writepw       fopen /etc/passwd "write", putpw, fclose
    075b  putpw         the list out, last node first, so oldest first
    086f  putgstr       fputs to the group file
    0886  putstr        fputs to the passwd file
    089d  itoa          itob into a static buffer
    08c6  say           write(1, s, strlen(s))
    08ee  findname      a passwd node by login name
    0941  prompt        say, read(0, buf, 512), the newline off
    09cb  ignoresigs    signal(i, 1) for i from 1 to 14
    0a04  readgr        /etc/group into the list
    0b96  findgr        a group's number by name
    0bfb  grpname       UNREFERENCED - a name by number, or the number as text
    0c61  mkpath        mkdir -p, with mknod and two links
    0dd7  exists        stat, as a truth value
    0e04  dirname       into a static buffer
    0f0c  uidused       a passwd node by number
    0f89  newuid        the lowest free from 11, or 0
    0fd4  showgr        the group names, tab and newline
    10bf  askgroup      readgr, list, prompt, goodname, strsave
    1115  rootonly      getuid; if not zero, errno = EPERM, perror, exit(0)
    113d  addgr         copy a group into the list
    11f9  newgid        the lowest free from 1, or "Out of group numbers"
    12c3  writegr       if the list changed: fopen, putgr, fclose
    1318  putgr         the groups out, last node first
    13fa  lock          link(/etc/passwd, /etc/passwdL), and the touch
    14c8  unlock        unlink(/etc/passwdL)
    14d6  stale         the age of the lock, against sixty seconds
    1594  grpnum        a group's number, making the group if there is none

The strings sit between the functions and are in newuser.ctl.  The messages
are all in the binary and are all in newuser.c, word for word.

Two of them are not called.  removeuser and grpname are in the text and
nothing references them, though the strings they use are all there, so they
were once part of a different or larger program - an earlier newuser that
could also remove an account is the obvious reading.  They are not carried
over; newuser.c's header says so.


what it does, in the order it does it
--------------------------------------

main calls rootonly, which is getuid and a compare against zero: any other
user is told "Not super-user" and stopped.  The binary is mode 4711, so it
can be run by anyone and refuses everyone but root.

getname prompts "Name of user: " until goodname is satisfied.  adduser then
works on a twelve byte passwd record on its own stack - the same twelve
bytes as <pwd.h>'s struct passwd: name 0, passwd 2, uid 4 and gid 5 as bytes,
person 6, dir 8, shell 10.

    name       the answer, strsaved
    group      askgroup: readgr, list the names, say what may be typed,
               prompt for one, hold it to goodname, strsave it
    dir        "/a/" and the name
    shell      /bin/sh;  passwd  the empty string
    person     "(This entry is optional." and a prompt, and every colon
               typed turned into a semicolon

Then ignoresigs, lock, readpw and readgr again - the group list is read a
second time because the first was before the lock - and the checks:

    a name already there is reported and the lock put down
    newuid is called - 11 up, the first no entry has - and then grpnum for
    the group, which may make one.  A uid of 0 is "no more", and the lock is
    put down.

The record has its two bytes filled, goes into the list, mkpath makes the
home directory, chown gives it away (the owner packed as uid | gid << 8, the
way owner.c does it), writepw writes /etc/passwd, writegr writes /etc/group
if the list grew, and "New user added." is said.  The lock goes down at the
end.

The lock itself is the one odd piece and the one most worth saying how it
was read.  14d6 is stat, time, and a subtraction of two longs, with the
absolute value taken if it came out negative, compared with 60.  13fa is a
loop:

    if stale(lock) unlink(lock)
    if link(/etc/passwd, lock) < 0: sleep(1), again
    fd = open(lock, 2); read one byte; seek to 0; write it back; close
    if the lock exists: done, else sleep(1), again

The lock is a hard link to the password file, so there is one inode under
both names; writing a byte through the lock's name stamps that inode's
modification time with now, which is what stale reads.  It is a lock whose
age is the time since it was taken, kept in the file it protects.  The link
is the test-and-set, and the stale check is the only thing that gets a
crashed run out of the way.  The binary does not unlink after a fopen
failure - it just exits, and the lock is left to age out - which is the one
thing the port does differently on purpose.

The long arithmetic is Whitesmith's, in the order the library keeps it: the
high word first, each word low byte first.  The sign of a long is the byte at
offset 1.  The constant sixty is stored as 00 00 3c 00, which is what that
layout makes of it.


what the port does not do as the binary did
-------------------------------------------

The exit status was inverted.  main ends with exit(1), every error path ends
with exit(0), and the library's exit passes its argument straight through, so
a successful run exits 1 and a refused one exits 0.  Whitesmith's own tools
spelled the two exit(YES) and exit(NO), and exit(YES) is the likely source
text here; nothing in this binary's library turns them into 0 and 1.  The duplicate name path calls exit with no
argument at all, which leaves whatever was in the slot.  The port exits 0 on
success and 1 on anything else.

The prompt read does not notice an end of file.  read returns 0, the buffer is
the empty string, the empty string is not a name, and it asks again - for ever.
The port gives up.

Where the binary left the lock held when it could not open /etc/passwd or
/etc/group, the port takes it down before it exits.

Nothing else is changed: the same questions, the same messages, the same two
files, the same uid and gid ranges, the same home directory, the same lock.


how it was checked
------------------

newuser.c builds clean with this tree's ccc and links against libc.a and
libu.a with no undefined symbol: the image is 17589 bytes against the
binary's 17526.

It was run in usersim, as root, against a copy of the distribution's
/etc/passwd and /etc/group, a line at a time with a pause between the lines,
because a file or a pipe delivers the whole script to the first read where a
terminal delivers one line:

    a new name, a new group      uid 11, gid 6, the group appended to
                                 /etc/group, /a/bob made, the lock gone
    a colon in the comment       a semicolon in the file
    names of one letter, or with a capital      refused and asked again
    a name already there         reported, nothing written, lock gone
    an existing group, no comment               uid 12, gid 6, the group
                                 file left as it was
    a stale lock (ten minutes old)   broken, the run goes on
    a fresh lock                 waits, as the binary does

Not checked: the check against a user who is not root, because usersim's
getuid answers 0 whoever runs it; end of file on a terminal, because usersim's
terminal read does not deliver one (its sh has the same trouble); and the
native build - it has been built by the cross compiler and nothing else.

It is not installed.  The /bin/newuser in filesystem is still the
distribution's.
